                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-3",
                                    "linecount": 34,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 460.0, 510.0 ],
                                    "text": "ButterComp2 — Airwindows (Chris Johnson)\nPage: https://www.airwindows.com/buttercomp2/\n\nWhat it is: the Airwindows \"Butter\" compressor - an extremely squishy,\ndark, vintage-feeling feedback compressor with no threshold/ratio/attack\nknobs. Everything is driven by an input gain. Gain-cell slopes are quadratic\non the signal itself, so gain reduction follows the actual peak shape of the\naudio instead of an exact multiplication; the makeup gain is nonlinear, so\nthe depth of compression is baked into the audio rather than calculated.\nThis RNBO port uses the original normalized 0..1 values.\n\nParameters:\n\n- p_compress (Compress) 0..1, default 0.0\n  One knob: simultaneously input drive and feedback depth. Internally maps\n  to +14 dB input gain at max (pow(10, p*14/20)) with audio-dependent\n  feedback. Rough guides:\n  0.0-0.2   barely compresses (0 = bypass-ish, unity drive)\n  0.4-0.5   vintage \"glue\", pumping but musical\n  0.9-1.0   extreme limiting / pushes almost everything to a squashed mean\n  Nearly linear feel: each 0.1 up ≈ +1.4 dB drive. Default 0 means mostly\n  unused unless you push the knob.\n\n- p_output (Output) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Output trim, internally output * pow(2,p*2-1)... implemented as output*2\n  with p 0..1, so 0.5 = unity, 0 = -6 dB-like, 1 = +6 dB-like. Linear taper.\n\n- p_drywet (Dry/Wet) 0..1, default 1.0\n  Plain linear mix of compressed and dry signal (1.0 = full wet).\n\nMusical notes: put on the drum buss, rock out the Compress knob until it\npumps, then ride Output for level. The buttery \"bloom\" comes from the\nnonlinear makeup gain; with parallel compression use the Dry/Wet for\ntransient retention rather than trying to replace the compressed texture entirely.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
