                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-3",
                                    "linecount": 35,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 460.0, 525.0 ],
                                    "text": "Density3 — Airwindows (Chris Johnson)\nPage: https://www.airwindows.com/density3/\n\nWhat it is: the \"Density\" family compressor/limiter: a one-knob compressor\nwith a built-in expanding \"air\" highpass feeding the drive so low frequencies\ndon't overload it. The gain cell is sine-shaped, and above a certain drive it\ncreatively distorts (a soft \"densifying\" saturation) rather than simply\nsquashing. This RNBO port uses the original normalized 0..1 values.\n\nParameters:\n\n- p_density (Density) 0..1, default 0.0\n  Compression/drive intensity. Internally density = p*5, i.e. an input gain\n  of up to 5x (+14 dB equivalent) into the sine gain cell. Guides:\n  0.0-0.1    bypass / near-bypass (default is NOT bypass - see below)\n  0.2        light, converges to almost the source output (flat)\n  0.4-0.6    heavy musical \"densify\" with harmonic bloom\n  0.8-1.0    limiting with visible distortion artifacts - use on busses only\n\n- p_highpas (Highpas) 0..1, default 0.0\n  Tames sub-bass from loading the gain cell: it is a highpass filter at a\n  cubic taper (iirAmount = p^3 / sampleRate). 0 = off; higher = faster\n  highpass (effectively higher cutoff). Musically: on big drums / big bass\n  busses, raise it so kicks don't eat the limiter's range.\n\n- p_output (Output) 0..1, default 1.0\n  Linear output trim (unity at 1.0).\n\n- p_drywet (Dry/Wet) 0..1, default 1.0\n  Linear dry/wet blend.\n\nMusical notes: default is not bypass (see README): unity output happens at\np_density 0.2 with Highpas off; start there, push Density until the mix\n\"opens up\", then trim Output down. It's a master-buss tool first, but works\non single tracks when you want density instead of visible compression.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
