                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-3",
                                    "linecount": 54,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 460.0, 798.0 ],
                                    "text": "Ultralight2 — Airwindows (Chris Johnson)\nPage: https://www.airwindows.com/ultralight2/\n\nWhat it is: the most low-CPU Airwindows reverb possible: a 4x4 Householder\nmatrix plus tone shaping and a small allpass chain running beside the\npre-delay. It is made for dozens of mono instances in a game-like mix; on\nstereo it is a very honest \"plain decent room\". This RNBO port keeps float32\ndelay storage like the source, uses 10 sequential steps for some controls\n(see below), and uses multiply-add instead of a fused FMA (numerical note).\n\nParameters:\n\n- p_damping (Damping) 0..1, default 0.65 (10 discrete steps)\n  Internally index = floor(p*9.99), so the slider snaps to 10 levels.\n  damping = 0.0625 - (index * 0.003125), and index 0 = verb BYPASS\n  (the network is skipped entirely, you hear dry). Level 1 = most HF\n  damping going into the matrix (dark, close, cozy); level 9 (max) is\n  the brightest and most ringy.\n\n- p_unsolid (UnSolid) 0..1, default 0.75 (5 discrete steps)\n  Internal \"unsolid\" control: parametrizes 4 - floor(p*4.99). It reduces\n  the \"solid walls\" feeling by inserting slight modal variation in the\n  matrix lines. Low p values = more solid/metallic comb; high p values =\n  a more diffuse, wooden-sounding space. In-between values are steppy on\n  purpose - audition the 5 stops.\n\n- p_unreflc (UnReflc) 0..1, default 0.75 (5 discrete steps)\n  Internal \"unreflect\": reduces discrete early reflection / metallic echo\n  density the same 5-way way. 0 = very defined reflections, 1.0 = a\n  smooth, flat room blur.\n\n- p_predlay (Predlay) 0..1, default 0.54\n  Pre-delay to prime-indexed delay lines. 0 (roughly below 0.01) bypasses\n  the pre-delay chain; otherwise the index (about 1..998) selects four\n  prime-numbered delays, roughly up to ~20 ms at 44.1 kHz (with\n  additional sqrt-nested prime partners). Musically: small values on\n  drums keep transients intact, bigger values push the vocal back in\n  the space.\n\n- p_distnce (Distnce) 0..1, default 0.0\n  Extra perceived distance for the wet path, from the FastDistance idea:\n  internally distance = pow(p*7.28, 2) (about 0..53 steps), a quadratic\n  taper. Unlike a simple volume, it adds an interpolating depth-wobble\n  that lets you sit a source \"deeper\" in the room. Default 0 = off.\n\n- p_drywet (Dry/Wet) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Straight linear dry/wet blend.\n\nMusical notes: exactly what the name says: cheap, clean, a bit sterile.\nUse as the default \" glue-less room\" on a mono source, or layer multiple\nprograms for a bigger virtual hallscape (the reason it was written).\nThe 10-step Damping always picks a discrete value - use 0.65/0.75 as\nstarting points and trust the discrete stops rather than continuous\nlistening.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
