                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-3",
                                    "linecount": 46,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 460.0, 683.0 ],
                                    "text": "VerbTiny — Airwindows (Chris Johnson)\nPage: https://www.airwindows.com/verbtiny/\n\nWhat it is: an ultra-low-CPU artificial reverb built from a specially\nevolved 4x4 matrix, meant to merge with the sound and change its texture\n(that \"tssshhh\" of a naive digital reverb, in a good way) rather than to\nsimulate a physical room. Intentionally \"old school\". This RNBO port uses\nthe original normalized 0..1 values.\n\nParameters:\n\n- p_replace (Replace) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Regeneration: the Galactic-style \"replace\" control. Above 0 it replaces\n  incoming dry signal with already-reverberated signal in the feedback\n  path, so the tail is \"refilled\" with the mixture. Lower values sound\n  like a light small room; the high end gets dense/ringy washes. Do not\n  push too hard: unlike your complex DSP reverb, at >0.9 it can saturate.\n\n- p_derez (Derez) 0..1, default 1.0\n  Darkens the reverb through a Bezier undersampling: it is a sample-rate\n  style reduction, not a normal filter - at higher values the wet\n  becomes \"aliased-ish\", \"grittier\", like a digital early digital reverb.\n  Default 1.0 is full effect without extra undersampling artifacts\n  (1.0 = no reduction; lowering it starts the dull and washed sound).\n\n- p_filter (Filter) 0..1, default 1.0\n  Tone of the wet reverb via a Bezier filter. 1.0 = brightest; lowering\n  makes a smooth tonal (not just lowpass) darker \"muffled room\".\n  Pairs with Derez for that old-school muddy-to-hi-fi scale.\n\n- p_wider (Wider) 0..1, default 0.0\n  Stereo image of the wet signal, with a few discrete worlds:\n  0.0-0.5 blends the normal narrow stereo reverb with a dual-mono variant\n  (richer, weirder mid-image \"unnatural wideness\" around 0.5);\n  0.5-1.0 then transitions into a HYPER-wide opposite-phase variant.\n  Try stops at 0.3, 0.5, 0.7 and listen for the mids changing shape\n  before the overall width does.\n\n- p_drywet (Dry/Wet) 0..1, default 1.0\n  Normal linear dry/wet blend (1.0 = full wet return).\n\nMusical notes: put on a return for dub/techno \"big tssshhh\", or on single\ntracks where you want the verb to melt into the tone. The Wider control is\nthe character knob: avoid 0.5 exactly if you want natural stereo.\nNote: Trash (the VerbThic sibling) shares the same 5 controls but is\na different - denser, stubbier - matrix: see VerbThic.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
