                            },
                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-3",
                                    "linecount": 65,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 460.0, 957.0 ],
                                    "text": "X2Buss — Airwindows (Chris Johnson)\nPage: https://www.airwindows.com/x2buss/\n\nWhat it is: the \"X\"-generation 2-buss: four bands of tone control (High,\nHiMid, LoMid, Bass) done with nonlinear (x|x|x) biquads and a soft-clip\nclipper stage on the output (the ADClip filter family, shared with\nZAcidLowpass / ZOutputStage tone stages). Tone is musical \"console turn the\nknob a little\" rather than surgical EQ. This RNBO port uses the original\nnormalized 0..1 values; note the port allocates the correct 33/65 slew\nbuffers (README note: the source would otherwise overrun).\n\nParameters:\n\n- p_high (High) 0..1, default 0.5\n  High-band gain. Centered at 0.5 (= flat); each side is a mild\n  nonlinear x|x|x boost/cut, maximum about +/-6 dB, curved (not dB-taper\n  but nonlinear gain factor 1 + x*|x|*|x| with x = 2(p-0.5)). Small moves\n  sound great, big moves get a bit \"solid-state\".\n\n- p_hmid (HMid) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Same taper for the high-mid shelf/peak band.\n\n- p_lmid (LMid) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Same taper for the low-mid shelf band.\n\n- p_bass (Bass) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Same taper for the bass shelf band.\n\n- p_highf (HighF) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Reference frequency adjust for the high band (0.5 = neutral). It moves\n  the high corner from its default up or down, modulated also by the\n  neighboring bands' references, so this is a \"family\" control rather\n  than an absolute Hz readout.\n\n- p_hmidf (HMidF) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Same idea for the high-mid band, which also sets the Q of the high band\n  (inter-band interaction, by design: treble and hi-mid lean on each\n  other).\n\n- p_lmidf (LMidF) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Low-mid reference; affects the low-mid band and the mid Q.\n\n- p_bassf (BassF) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Bass reference; also affects the low Q. Bands with 0.5/0.5/0.5/0.5 keep\n  the classic X2 reference points.\n\nAll four (p_highf, p_hmidf, p_lmidf, p_bassf) are done with smooth\nnonlinear reference shifts rather than octave-week straight jumps, so\nmove them in small steps for natural tone.\n\n- p_thresh (Thresh) 0..1, default 1.0\n  Output clipper ceiling, with ADClip-style noise for reconstructing most\n  one-sample top shapes; 1.0 = clipper at the limits (mostly passive),\n  lower settings progressively squish and distort the buss mix. Very\n  steep: small downward moves already matter.\n\n- p_fader (Fader) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Master gain/fader on the output, linear 0..1; 0.5 = unity by convention\n  inside the source (like a real console fader).\n\nMusical notes: this is a console-flavored tone + safety buss. Use the four\ntone controls around 0.45-0.6 for \"console glue\" moves and Thresh below\n1.0 only when the mix pushes - it eats a lot of level when engaged.\nBeware the internal reference-frequency interaction: raise BassF if the\nBass shelf sits around the kick, or it'll lose instead of gain punch.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
