                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-3",
                                    "linecount": 100,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 460.0, 1461.0 ],
                                    "text": "Chmahastic Reverb 2 — mrelwood plugins\nPage: https://mrelwood5.wixsite.com/plugins\n(Chmahastic Reverb has no individual page; it is listed under Others in\nthe mrelwood plugin list and ships inside the free mrelwood_PluginPack.\nDSP is built on Airwindows' Verbity2 / Verbity (MIT), wrapped as a JSFX\nand enlarged for scratch-modelling / denser per-program spaces.)\n\nWhat it is: a very versatile feedback-matrix reverb: a 5x5 delay network\n(50 delay/feedback lines from Verbity) with nonlinear feedback, \"input\nattack\" driving the tail, undersampling with interpolation, plus a full\ntone-shaping section (3-band shelves EQ, lowpass, highpass) sized for\nmaking anything from tiny rooms to enormous baths. It does not have a\nconventional decay algorithm - the relationships between Decay, Room,\nDampen and Attack is where the character lives. This RNBO port keeps the\nwhole 50-cross-feedback nonlinear network and enlarges the RAM so the\nsource's overlapping memory bug (right amount of 8% extra length) is\nREPAIRED rather than emulated (README note). RAM is 160000 doubles.\n\nParameters (original names):\n\n- slider1 (Room Size) % 0..100, default 60\n  Overall sense of space. Roughly correlates with how much delay line\n  depth the network grows; bigger = more \"smear\", smaller = drier,\n  closer, more slap. The relationship between Room Size and Decay is\n  the main character dial here, not an absolute cubic meter readout.\n\n- slider2 (Decay) % 0..100, default 50\n  How the tail energy decays: lower = early-energy only / dead,\n  mid-values = musical room tail, 80+ = long washes suitable to things\n  like drums and pads. Because it interacts with Dampen and Room Size,\n  two identical Decay values with different Dampen sound completely\n  different - always compare against yourself by ear.\n\n- slider3 (Dampen) % 0..100, default 40\n  HF absorption in the network: 0 = bright metallic (think genre\n  film \"hall\" cliches), 40-60 = natural air, 80+ = classic dark\n  diffusion giving that drum-room \"thick\" about. Uses the source's\n  damping with random (not REAPER) RNG - see README.\n\n- slider4 (Pre-Delay) (ms) 0..100, default 30\n  Classic pre-delay. The source keeps a 4800-sample ceiling, giving\n  roughly 105 ms at 44.1 kHz. Remember: in the port, changes clear the\n  pre-delay buffers (README note), so expect a small click-free swell\n  when you move it.\n\n- slider5 (Input Attack) % 0..100, default 90\n  How the tail \"swells up\" under real program material. High values =\n  the verb is fed from the start (lively, attack-heavy input), low\n  values = the river washes in with a slower, \"breathing\" onset. This\n  is the signature Chmahastic control: 80-100 for punchy drums, 0-30\n  for pad/tape-like builds.\n\n- slider6 (Low-Pass Frequency) (kHz) 1..20, default 8\n  A straight lowpass on the wet. The default 8 kHz is generally a\n  \"hi-fi hall\" sound; 4-6 kHz for darker/behind vocal tracks; 20 kHz\n  starts to get crisp/hissy bright.\n\n- slider7 / slider8 (Hi-Mid Gain dBad / Frequency) -9..+6 dB /\n  1000..8000 Hz, defaults 0 dB / 3500 Hz\n  A bell/shelf-ish emphasis on the high-mids of the wet (clarity of\n  the tail). +2..+3 dB at 2-4 kHz is the \"audibility\" move; pushing\n  high makes cymbal-verb fizzle.\n\n- slider9 / slider10 (Mid Gain / Frequency) -9..+6 dB /\n  300..5000 Hz, defaults 0 dB / 1000 Hz\n  The body of the tail: 1000 Hz is a good \"mid-body\" ref. Negative here\n  sinks the reverb behind the mix without getting muddy.\n\n- slider11 / slider12 (Lo-Mid Gain / Frequency) -9..+6 dB /\n  150..2000 Hz, defaults 0 dB / 200 Hz\n  Weight of the low end of the tail: negative values keep a big\n  \"airy\" hall from crowding the kick; positive values are for\n  super-large \"cathedral\" grows.\n\n- slider13 (High-Pass Frequency) (Hz) 50..1000, default 50\n  Highpass of the wet (bass trap). 50 Hz default = essentially full\n  bass; 100-300 Hz is where pro mix engineers set their big verb\n  returns so they don't arc-weld the low end.\n\n- slider14 (Stereo Width) % -150..+150, default 120\n  Negative values = narrower / mono-ish (and inverted at extreme\n  negatives), 100 = full stereo, 150 = hyper-wide. Musically 60-120\n  is the working zone; push negative on drum-heavy programs so tails\n  don't drift outside the kick width.\n\n- slider15 (Mix) % 0..100, default 100\n  0..1 wet cross-fade. NOTE: at slider15 = 0 the DRY output still has\n  the source's mix-law attenuation - it is NOT a unity bypass\n  (README note).\n\n- slider16 (Volume) (dB) -30..+9, default 0\n  Straight wet output trim. Use it to compensate the swap between\n  small / large spaces so your return level stays consistent.\n\nPort quirks (README notes): the 50-line cross-feedback and nonlinear\nfeedback have been preserved in full; RAM was enlarged to prevent the\nsource's memory overlap; damping random uses RNBO random rather than\nREAPER's implementation; the commented-out high-pass variable stays\nzero; and slider15's dry law behaves exactly as in the source (not a\nbypass). Listen after the full tail has developed (several seconds).\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
