                            {
                                "box": {
                                    "id": "obj-3",
                                    "linecount": 102,
                                    "maxclass": "comment",
                                    "numinlets": 1,
                                    "numoutlets": 0,
                                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 460.0, 1490.0 ],
                                    "text": "GoldComp 4 — mrelwood plugins\nPage: https://mrelwood5.wixsite.com/plugins\n(GoldComp4 has no individual page on the site; it is listed and distributed\ninside the free mrelwood_PluginPack.)\n\nWhat it is: mrelwood's \"latest go-to compressor for everything\": a stereo\n(or M/S) program compressor with knee, ratio, attack/release, an\nFET-vs-opto release flavor switch, detector HP, oversampling selector (the\nsource's 6 detector paths), RMS-target makeup, negative (upward) expansion\nand a built-in limiter on the output. This RNBO port keeps all of the\nsource's detector paths; it does NOT oversample the waveform itself\n(README note). Parameters use the original JSFX slider units.\n\nParameters (original names; slider1 \"Stereo\", slider2..21 in file):\n\n- slider2  (Stereo: L/R - M/S) 0..1, default 0.5\n  0 = plain stereo processing, 1 = mid/side processing. Beyond ~0.9 the\n  source effectively always uses M/S curves; near 0.5 the crossover is\n  smooth. Musically: M/S on program material, L/R on sparse/single\n  sources.\n\n- slider3  (L/R Link) 0..1, default 0.7\n  How much the two channels' detectors are linked. 0 = fully\n  independent (chases wide images), 1 = fully linked (no image shift).\n  Default 0.7 is a good mix glue setting.\n\n- slider4  (Side thr offset) -15..0, default -6 dB\n  In M/S mode, the amount by which the side channel detector is offset\n  relative to mid. Negative = side compressed earlier/harder, which\n  tightens wide imaging - useful on stems like drum overheads.\n\n- slider5  (Threshold) -48..0, default 0 dB\n  Detector threshold in dBFS. 0 = no compression (limiter off); -6\n  start of normal crushing territory.\n\n- slider7  (Attack) 0.02..50, default 5 ms\n  Attack time per 12 dB of GR (labelled \"ms/12dB\" in the source). Log\n  taper built into the detector: short attacks (<5 ms) let\n  transients through fairly free; long attacks give that classic\n  soft-knee squash. 0.02 ms is essentially instant.\n\n-  slider8 (Ratio) 1..12, default 4\n  Compression ratio (ratio 1 = off). Above ~8 approaches hard limiting,\n  below 3 is gentle leveling.\n\n- slider9  (Release) 10..2000, default 120 ms\n  Release time per 12 dB of GR. Pre-120 is quick punch recovery, past\n  500-1000 pumps are slow and梯 continuous (great for glue,\n  dangerous on fast material).\n\n- slider10 (Opto Release, FET - Opto) 0..1, default 1\n  Release character: 0 = FET-style fast/synthety independent recovery,\n  1 = Opto-style program-dependent slower release. Discrete switch, not\n  tapered.\n\n- slider11 (OverSampling) 0..6, default 0\n  The source's internally available detector oversampling selection\n  (0 = no extra detector paths active). This RNBO port faithfully\n  retains all 7 numeric detector configurations - although, as noted,\n  it does NOT oversample audio itself (README).\n\n- slider12 (RMS Target) -36..-6, default -24 dB\n  The original calculated makeup gain target, NOT a new RMS controller\n  (README note). Higher (closer to -6) = very high calculated makeup\n  (louder bass result), lower/-36 keeps output near unity.\n\n- slider13 (Output Trim) -24..+24, default 0 dB\n  Straight output level trim in dB.\n\n- slider15 (Limiter) 0..1, default 1 (off in the source, on in the port)\n  Output limiter stage. NOTE: the source uses 1 = OFF for this control\n  (it is an off/on switch; the port default 1 leaves it off). When on\n  it catches the remaining overshoots of the compressor/limiter path -\n  handy when pushing threshold very low.\n\n- slider16 (Detector HP) 20..1000 Hz, default 20\n  Highpass of the sidechain detector. 20 Hz = full-band detection;\n  50-150 Hz removes the kick/bass from detection so the compressor\n  doesn't over-react to synth bass; 500-1000 turns it into a\n  vocal/brightness-only detector.\n\n- slider17 (Mix) 0..100, default 100 %\n  Dry/wet style parallel LeCompt blend of compressed and dry audio.\n\n- slider20 (Tilt EQ) -12..+12, default 0 dB\n  Built-in wide tilt shelves on the audio buss (not on the detector).\n  Positive = brighter/tilted up, negative = darker/tilted down. Not a\n  replacement for an EQ - keep it subtle (+/- 1-2 dB).\n\n- slider21 (NegExp, Ratio) 1..4, default 1\n  Negative (upward) expansion ratio. Above 1 it lifts very quiet\n  passages, which makes an interesting \"thinner but detailed\" master.\n  Leave at 1 for a plain compressor; use 2-4 for subtle \"loudrooms\n  dreaminess\" or floor noise lifting.\n\nPort quirks (README notes): the width slider label in the source's GR\ndisplay and its related meters are omitted; slider13 output trim and\nslider12 RMS target control the original calculated makeup. The attdb\nvariable in the source is zero (its assignment line is commented out) and\nits wide tilt shelves, detector HP, negative expansion and post-mix\nlimiter are all retained in the port. slider17 = 0 with limiter off\npasses dry audio.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
