                                    "text": "HP-LP LR4 Filters — mrelwood plugins (third-party contribution)\nPage: https://mrelwood5.wixsite.com/plugins\n(This filter has no individual page; it is a third-party LR4 contribution\nlisted with mrelwood's plugins and ships inside the free\nmrelwood_PluginPack. Parameters keep the original JSFX slider units.)\n\nWhat it is: a standalone Linkwitz-Riley-style crossover / HP / LP filter\nbank. It has FOUR outputs in the RNBO port: when Mode is set to\nCrossover, out1/out2 are the LOW-passed audio and out3/out4 are the\nHIGH-passed audio, so you can wire them into two speaker/monitor paths.\nSelectors keep the source's numeric values, including its oddities\n(README note: selector 3 uses seven cascaded biquads rather than the\nlabelled twelve poles; 4/5 use seven LP and four HP stages - the source's\nown imperfections are preserved, not \"fixed\" into a textbook crossover).\n\nParameters (original names):\n\n- slider2 (Slope selector) 0..5, default 1\n  Numeric pole/slope selector. Discrete steps. 0 = straight passthrough.\n  Higher numbers use progressively more cascaded biquads (steeper slopes,\n  more phase rotation). Selector 1 is the plainest working crossover.\n\n- slider3 (Frequency Scale) 0..1, default 0.5\n  Sets the crossover / HP / LP frequency. It is a SCALE control with a\n  log-ish mapping inside the source, not a direct Hz readout: 0.5 is the\n  central musical range (roughly 300-400 Hz region for typical uses),\n  lower = bassier / lower cutoffs, higher = higher, sharper splits.\n\n- slider4 (Resonance) (dB) -6..+6, default 0\n  Resonance on the filter bank. 0 = proper LR-style flat crossover;\n  negative pulls the passband down (gentler handoff), positive lets the\n  corner ring a little (musical for a filter-y \"swoop\"). Keep small\n  values to preserve the classic LR4 cancelled-notch behavior.\n\n- slider5 (LP Polarity) 0..1 (0/1 toggle), default 0\n  Inverts the OUTPUT of the low pass filter. Use it if the phase of the\n  crossover audibly fights the source; the standard setup for a two-way\n  LR-type crossover is either both flipped (right for 24 dB/LR4\n  acoustic alignment) or both NOT flipped - flipping one gives the\n  famous sub-bass cancellation \"swoop\".\n\n- slider6 (HP Polarity) 0..1 (0/1 toggle), default 0\n  Same polarity toggle for the high-pass outputs.\n\n- slider8 (Mode) 0..3, default 0\n  0 = off (plain stereo passthrough),\n  1 = LP only (high band is NOT output on out3/out4),\n  2 = HP only (low band is NOT output on out1/out2),\n  3 = crossover (both: out1/out2 = LOW, out3/out4 = HIGH).\n  Only in Mode 3 do the extra HP outlets carry working audio.\n\n- slider20 (Output) (dB) -24..+24, default 0\n  Straight output trim applied to both bands.\n\nPort quirks (README notes): Off mode (slider8 = 0) passes stereo at\nunity; crossover outputs are never auto-summed within the module, so\nyou connect them yourself. The source's passband numerical selectors\nare all preserved as-is.\n",
                                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                                }
                            }
                        ],
